```python
def quicksort_hoare(arr, lo, hi):
    if lo < hi:
        p = hoare_partition(arr, lo, hi)
        quicksort_hoare(arr, lo, p)      # note: includes p
        quicksort_hoare(arr, p + 1, hi)

def hoare_partition(arr, lo, hi):
    pivot = arr[lo]
    i, j = lo - 1, hi + 1
    while True:
        i += 1
        while arr[i] < pivot:
            i += 1
        j -= 1
        while arr[j] > pivot:
            j -= 1
        if i >= j:
            return j
        arr[i], arr[j] = arr[j], arr[i]
```

The recursive calls use `[lo, p]` and `[p + 1, hi]` — **including** `p` in the left call. That's the detail that breaks if you copy-paste Lomuto's `[lo, p - 1]` / `[p + 1, hi]` split onto Hoare's returned index (slide 12).
